{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0e774dcd47ba61490169bb1754cd288191a34d2842e1792e30", "created": 1773868884, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "**LIVER** is a large digestive-system organ located on the right side under the rib cage, with right and left lobes and a central role in bile flow anatomy (the pancreas sits just below it). It filters blood and removes toxins, produces bile to help break down fats, processes sugar for energy, helps with blood clotting by making coagulation factors, contains blood and lymph vessels, and produces lymph. **LIVER** health and function are commonly monitored with blood tests—especially liver function tests (often within a comprehensive metabolic panel/chemistry profile) that measure enzymes and substances such as alkaline phosphatase (ALP) and bilirubin; abnormal results can suggest liver damage, bile duct blockage, swelling/enlargement from cancer-cell buildup, or possible cancer spread. **LIVER** is also monitored during treatments such as AML therapy and recovery, is considered when selecting systemic therapy, and can be damaged as a serious side effect of cancer treatment (including toxicity from high-dose methotrexate). It is the organ affected by hepatitis B/C infection and may be harmed by hepatitis virus reactivation during cancer treatment; it is also associated with hepatitis, cirrhosis, and primary liver cancer.\n\nIn oncology, **LIVER** is frequently referenced as a distant organ site evaluated during staging and metastatic workups (often by CT, MRI, or other imaging) and may be sampled/biopsied for tumor testing. **LIVER** is described as a common or possible metastatic site for many cancers, including esophageal cancer, colon cancer (a common site in stage 4 disease), breast cancer (including as an example “M” site in stage 4), anal cancer (a common distant site in stage IV, assessed with CT chest/abdomen; metastatic disease is typically treated first-line with systemic chemotherapy), thyroid cancer, bladder cancer (including stage 4B metastatic disease), stomach cancer (a common and frequent spread site), pancreatic cancer (advanced disease commonly spreads to the liver), ovarian cancer (can spread to the liver surface in stage 3C or to the inside in stage 4B), prostate cancer (a possible spread site in advanced disease), and cancers such as rectal cancer (evaluated by CT/MRI for spread). **LIVER** is also listed as a possible site for tumors in DLBCL and as a potential site of leukemia spread outside the bloodstream. It is sometimes referenced to distinguish locally advanced from metastatic cervical cancer when it has *not* spread to the **LIVER**. In the metastatic setting, **LIVER** lesions may be candidates for local therapies such as stereotactic body radiation therapy (SBRT) in selected cases, including metastatic cervical cancer.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 551, "prompt_tokens": 1461, "total_tokens": 2012, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 0, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}